>> title: Shaved Brussels Salad
>> category: Salads
>> tags: brussels sprouts, salad, pecorino, hazelnuts, lemon, no-cook
>> counters: The Bowl Shop
>> aka: shaved brussels sprout salad, brussel sprout salad, shredded brussels sprouts, raw brussels salad, sprout slaw, everyday kale and brussels salad
>> pairs-with: kale-caesar
>> servings: 4
>> time: 30 min
>> slack: forgiving — shaved sprouts sit in lemon for an hour and get better; only the nuts lose anything, and they lose it slowly
>> step.1: toast 8 min, then rub the skins off
>> step.2: slice paper thin
>> step.3: stand 15 min in the lemon
>> step.4: crumble in

Toast @hazelnuts{3/4%cup}(100 g) in a dry #skillet{} over medium heat for ~toast{8%min}, shaking the pan, then tip them into a towel and rub the skins off while they are hot. Skinned or not is not a fussiness question — hazelnut skin is bitter and it is the one bitter thing this salad has no use for, since the sprouts have already brought their own.

Slice @Brussels sprouts{1%lb}(450 g; trimmed, halved through the root) paper thin on a #mandoline{}, cut side down. Thin enough to see through is the whole technique: a sprout sliced thick is a small cabbage and stays a small cabbage, and one sliced thin falls apart into ribbons that behave like a leaf.

Stand @&(~1)shaved sprouts{} in @lemon juice{3%Tbs}(45 mL) and @kosher salt{3/4%tsp} for ~stand{15%min}, turning them over once. The acid does here what massaging does to kale, without a hand in the bowl — fifteen minutes and the raw cabbage edge is gone.

Crumble @&(~3)toasted hazelnuts{} into @&(~1)sprouts{} with @pecorino{2%oz}(60 g; broken in shards), @extra-virgin olive oil{1/4%cup}(60 mL), @honey{1%tsp} and @black pepper{1/2%tsp}.

The dressing is made in the bowl and there is no jar of it in the drawer, because there could not be: it is the lemon the sprouts have been sitting in, loosened with oil once the salting is done. Add the oil at the start instead and the salt never gets into the sprouts at all.
